CONTENT='Title: PCIe Physical Layer Deep Dive - Gen1 to Gen7

Section 1: PCIe Protocol Overview
- PCIe uses a layered protocol architecture: Transaction Layer (L3), Data Link Layer (L2), Physical Layer (L1)
- Transaction Layer handles TLP packets (MRd/MWr/Cpl) with 6 flow control credits
- Data Link Layer ensures reliable delivery via LCRC, ACK/NACK, and Replay mechanism
- Physical Layer manages SerDes, encoding, FEC, and Flit framing

Section 2: Physical Layer Sub-Modules
- RPLH (Receive Physical Layer): deframing, ordered set parsing, link status
- SPLH (Send Physical Layer): framing, ordered set generation
- XPLH (Physical Layer Control): link training, speed negotiation, width configuration
- SerDes Interface: PAM4/NRZ encoding, high-speed differential signaling
- Generic PHY: PHY abstraction layer, PIPE interface adaptation

Section 3: Encoding Evolution - 8b/10b to 1b/1b
- Gen1-2 (8b/10b): 8-bit data encoded to 10-bit symbol, 20% overhead, 2.5-5.0 GT/s
- Gen3-5 (128b/130b): 128-bit data with 2-bit sync header, 1.54% overhead, 8-32 GT/s
- Gen6-7 (1b/1b): Each 1b carries 1 encoded bit via PAM4, ~0% overhead, 64-128 GT/s
- Encoding overhead reduction: 20% to 1.54% to near 0%
- Error correction moves from encoding to FEC

Section 4: PAM4 Signaling
- NRZ (Non-Return-to-Zero): 2 voltage levels, 1 bit per symbol
- PAM4 (Pulse Amplitude Modulation 4-level): 4 voltage levels, 2 bits per symbol
- Gen6-7 use PAM4 at 64-128 GT/s for doubled bandwidth
- Trade-off: reduced noise margin, requires FEC for error correction
- TX FIR Equalizer: pre-emphasis to compensate channel loss

Section 5: FEC Forward Error Correction
- Gen6: FEC adds 8B per 256B FLIT, protects against burst errors
- Gen7: Enhanced FEC with stronger error correction capability
- Three-layer protection: FEC (physical) + CRC (data link) + SeqNum (replay)
- FEC reduces effective bandwidth by ~3% but enables higher data rates

Section 6: 256B Flit Mode
- Fixed 256B frame structure: 6B Header + 236B Payload + 8B FEC + 6B CRC
- Flit types: Data Flit, idle Flit, Ordered Set Flit
- Flit Mode enables efficient high-speed transmission
- Non-Flit Mode: traditional symbol-based transmission for backward compatibility

Section 7: LTSSM State Machine
- Detect: Check for receiver presence via voltage detection
- Polling: Bit/symbol lock via TS1/TS2 exchange
- Configuration: Width negotiation, Lane number assignment
- L0: Normal operating state, active data transfer
- Recovery: Re-training for speed change or equalization
- L0s/L1/L2: Progressive low-power states
- Rate change flow: Send EIEOS then Recovery.Speed then switch PHY rate then re-lock then return to L0

Section 8: PCIe 7.0 Key Innovations
- 128 GT/s per lane with PAM4 signaling
- 1b/1b encoding with FEC protection
- TS0 Ordered Set for link training
- 4-Phase equalization for channel optimization
- Enhanced FLIT Mode with improved efficiency
- Backward compatible with Gen1-6 devices'

