title: "Network Emulation"
source: "system/devices/net.rst"
---

# Network Emulation

QEMU can simulate several network cards (e.g. PCI or ISA cards on the PC target) and can connect them to a network backend on the host or an emulated hub.

## Using TAP Network Interfaces

This is the standard way to connect QEMU to a real network. QEMU adds a virtual network device on your host (called `tapN`), and you can then configure it as if it was a real ethernet card.

### Linux Host

A distribution will generally provide specific helper scripts when it packages QEMU. By default these are found at `/etc/qemu-ifup` and `/etc/qemu-ifdown`. You must verify that your host kernel supports the TAP network interfaces: the device `/dev/net/tun` must be present.

### Windows Host

There is a virtual ethernet driver for Windows 2000/XP systems, called TAP-Win32. It is part of OpenVPN package, so download OpenVPN from https://openvpn.net/.

## Using the User Mode Network Stack

By using the option `-net user` (default configuration if no `-net` option is specified), QEMU uses a completely user mode network stack.

Virtual network configuration:

```
guest (10.0.2.15)  <------>  Firewall/DHCP server <-----> Internet
                              |          (10.0.2.2)
                              |
                              ---->  DNS server (10.0.2.3)
                              |
                              ---->  SMB server (10.0.2.4)
```

The QEMU VM behaves as if it was behind a firewall which blocks all incoming connections. The DHCP server assigns addresses starting from 10.0.2.15.

ICMP traffic in general does not work with user mode networking. `ping` to the local router (10.0.2.2) shall work. To allow ping for GID 100:
```
echo 100 100 > /proc/sys/net/ipv4/ping_group_range
```

When using `-netdev user,hostfwd=...`, TCP, UDP or UNIX connections can be redirected from the host to the guest.

## Using passt as the User Mode Network Stack

passt can be used as a simple replacement for SLIRP (`-net user`). passt doesn't require any capability or privilege, has better performance, full IPv6 support and better security.

Usage:
```
qemu-system-x86_64 [...OPTIONS...] -nic passt
qemu-system-x86_64 [...OPTIONS...] -net nic -net passt,tcp-ports=10001,udp-ports=10001
```

### Migration of passt

When passt is connected via the vhost-user interface it can be migrated with QEMU and network connections are not interrupted.

## Hubs

QEMU can simulate several hubs. Connect guest NICs or host network backends to a hub using `-netdev hubport` or `-nic hubport` options.

## Connecting Emulated Networks Between QEMU Instances

Using `-netdev socket` (or `-nic socket` or `-net socket`), it is possible to create emulated networks that span several QEMU instances.
ENDOFFILE
