# Boot Devices on s390x

## Booting with bootindex Parameter

For classical mainframe guests, always mark the boot device with the `bootindex` property:

```
qemu-system-s390x -drive if=none,id=dr1,file=guest.qcow2 \
                  -device virtio-blk,drive=dr1,bootindex=1
```

Multiple devices may have a bootindex. The lowest bootindex is tried first. The old-fashioned `-boot order=...` is not supported on s390x.

For CD-ROM boot, use `scsi-cd`:

```
qemu-system-s390x -blockdev file,node-name=c1,filename=... \
                  -device virtio-scsi \
                  -device scsi-cd,drive=c1,bootindex=1
```

## Booting without bootindex

The s390-ccw bios has rudimentary scanning support, but always specifying `bootindex` is recommended.

## Selecting Kernels with loadparm

```
qemu-system-s390x -machine s390-ccw-virtio,loadparm=<string>
```

- `PROMPT`: Show list of installed kernels
- `0-31`: Boot the kernel associated with the given number

Per-device `loadparm` can override the machine `loadparm`.

## Network Boot

```
qemu-system-s390x -netdev user,id=n1,tftp=...,bootfile=... \
                  -device virtio-net-ccw,netdev=n1,bootindex=1
```

Supports pxelinux.cfg-style configuration files.
ENDOFFILE)
