title: "GDB Usage"
source: "system/gdb.rst"
---

# GDB Usage

QEMU supports working with gdb via gdb's remote-connection facility (the "gdbstub"). This allows you to debug guest code in the same way that you might with a low-level debug facility like JTAG on real hardware.

## Basic Usage

Launch QEMU with `-s` and `-S` options:
- `-s` - Listen for gdb connection on TCP port 1234
- `-S` - Don't start the guest until told to from gdb

```
qemu-system-x86_64 -s -S -kernel bzImage -drive file=rootdisk.img,format=raw -append "root=/dev/sda"
```

Connect gdb:
```
> gdb vmlinux
(gdb) target remote localhost:1234
(gdb) c
```

## Useful Tips

1. `info reg` - Display all CPU registers
2. `x/10i $eip` - Display code at PC position
3. `set architecture i8086` - Dump 16 bit code

## Breakpoint and Watchpoint Support

For TCG system emulation, an infinite number of hardware assisted breakpoints and watchpoints are advertised. For other accelerators it depends on support. TCG cannot track all memory accesses in user-mode, so no watchpoint support there.

## Debugging Multicore Machines

GDB supports multiple "inferiors" (CPU clusters) each with multiple "threads" (CPUs). Connect with `extended-remote` protocol for multi-cluster machines:

```
(gdb) target extended-remote localhost:1234
(gdb) add-inferior
(gdb) inferior 2
(gdb) attach 2
(gdb) set schedule-multiple on
```

## Using Unix Sockets

```
qemu-system-x86_64 -chardev socket,path=/tmp/gdb-socket,server=on,wait=off,id=gdb0 -gdb chardev:gdb0 -S

(gdb) target remote /tmp/gdb-socket
```

Requires gdb version 9.0 or newer.

## Advanced Debugging Options

### Single-Stepping Behaviour

- `maintenance packet qqemu.sstepbits` - Display MASK bits
- `maintenance packet qqemu.sstep` - Display current mask value
- `maintenance packet Qqemu.sstep=HEX_VALUE` - Change single step mask

### Examining Physical Memory

- `maintenance packet qqemu.PhyMemMode` - Check memory mode (0=virtual, 1=physical)
- `maintenance packet Qqemu.PhyMemMode:1` - Switch to physical memory
- `maintenance packet Qqemu.PhyMemMode:0` - Switch back to virtual memory

## Security Considerations

The GDB socket is not protected by authentication, authorization or encryption. It is the user's responsibility to ensure only authorized clients can connect.
ENDOFFILE
