title: "Xen HVM Guest Support"
source: "system/i386/xen.rst"
---

# Xen HVM Guest Support

KVM has support for hosting Xen guests, intercepting Xen hypercalls and event channel delivery. Using the split irqchip is mandatory.

## Setup

Enable Xen mode by setting the `xen-version` property:

```
qemu-system-x86_64 --accel kvm,xen-version=0x40011,kernel-irqchip=split
```

With virtual APIC support:
```
qemu-system-x86_64 --accel kvm,xen-version=0x40011,kernel-irqchip=split --cpu host,+xen-vapic
```

## Properties

- `xen-version` - Xen version in XENVER_version form (enables Xen support)
- `xen-evtchn-max-pirq` - Maximum PIRQs (default: 256)
- `xen-gnttab-max-frames` - Grant table frames (default: 64)

## Xen Paravirtual Devices

The Xen PCI platform device is enabled automatically. Paravirtual devices:

- `xen-console` - Xen console device
- `xen-net-device` - Xen network device (default NIC for Xen guests)
- Xen block devices via `-drive file=...,if=xen`

## Booting Xen PV Guests

Use the Xen PV shim:
```
qemu-system-x86_64 --accel kvm,xen-version=0x40011,kernel-irqchip=split \
   -chardev stdio,id=char0 -device xen-console,chardev=char0 \
   -display none -m 1G -kernel xen -initrd bzImage \
   -append "pv-shim console=xen,pv -- console=hvc0 root=/dev/xvda1" \
   -drive file=${GUEST_IMAGE},if=xen
```

## Host OS Requirements

Minimal: Linux v5.12+. Linux v5.17 added PIRQ acceleration. Linux v5.19 accelerated PV timers and IPIs.
ENDOFFILE
echo "Done: i386/xen.md")
